{"name":"Cinnamon Candle","hex":"#b15d63","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cinnamon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small evergreen tree native to Sri Lanka and southern India, Cinnamomum verum or Cinnamomum zeylanicum, belonging to the family Lauraceae.","Several related trees, notably the Indonesian cinnamon (Cinnamomum burmanni) and Chinese cinnamon or cassia (Cinnamomum aromaticum or Cinnamomum cassia)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing cinnamon, or having a cinnamon taste.","Of a yellowish-brown color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cinnamon"},{"term":"candle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light source consisting of a wick embedded in a solid, flammable substance such as wax, tallow, or paraffin.","The protruding, removable portion of a filter, particularly a water filter."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To observe the growth of an embryo inside (an egg), using a bright light source.","To dry (greenware) prior to the firing cycle, setting the kiln at 200° Celsius until all water is removed from the greenware."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/candle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cinnamon","blocks":[{"text":"Cinnamon is a spice obtained from the inner bark of several tree species from the genus Cinnamomum. Cinnamon is used mainly as an aromatic condiment and flavouring additive in a wide variety of cuisines, in particular sweet and savoury dishes such as biscuits, breakfast cereals, snack foods, bagels, teas, hot chocolate, and traditional foods. The characteristic aroma and flavour of cinnamon derive from its essential oil and principal component, cinnamaldehyde, as well as numerous other constituents, including eugenol."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Cinnamomum_verum_spices.jpg/330px-Cinnamomum_verum_spices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinnamon","wikidata":"Q28165"},{"title":"Candle","blocks":[{"text":"A candle is an ignitable wick embedded in wax, or another flammable solid substance such as tallow, that provides light, and in some cases, a fragrance. A candle can also provide heat or a method of keeping time. Candles have been used for over two millennia around the world, and were a significant form of indoor lighting until the invention of other types of light sources. Although electric light has largely made candle use nonessential for illumination, candles are still commonly used for functional, symbolic and aesthetic purposes and in specific cultural and religious settings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg/330px-LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle","wikidata":"Q12888135"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Cinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg/500px-Cinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Cinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg/1280px-Cinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Cinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg/500px-Cinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cinnamomum verum, from Koehler's Medicinal-Plants (1887)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACinnamomum_verum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-182.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or